# 5. Relationships Between Variables

---

## Learning Objectives

By the end of this section, students will be able to:

- Understand why studying relationships between variables is important.
- Visualize relationships using **Scatter Plots**.
- Measure relationships using **Correlation**.
- Interpret a **Correlation Matrix**.
- Understand **Pearson Correlation Coefficient**.
- Distinguish between **Correlation** and **Causation**.

---

# Why Study Relationships Between Variables?

Until now, we have analyzed each variable individually.

For example:

- What is the average price?
- What is the distribution of mileage?
- Are there outliers?

These analyses describe **one variable at a time**.

However, Machine Learning is primarily interested in **relationships** between variables.

Questions such as:

- Does a higher mileage lead to a lower price?
- Does engine size affect fuel consumption?
- Is there a relationship between vehicle age and price?

Understanding these relationships is the first step toward building predictive models.

---

# Scatter Plot

A **Scatter Plot** is one of the most useful visualization tools in Machine Learning.

It displays the relationship between **two numerical variables**.

Each point represents one observation.

Example:

- X-axis → Mileage
- Y-axis → AskPrice

Each point corresponds to one car.

---

## Example

```
Price
 ^
 |
 |                 •
 |            •
 |        •
 |    •
 | •
 +---------------------------->
            Mileage
```

As mileage increases, the price generally decreases.

This suggests a **negative relationship**.

---

# Creating a Scatter Plot


---
#%%
print(df.info())
#%%
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.figure(figsize=(7,5))

plt.scatter(
    df["Age"],
    df["AskPrice"],
    alpha=0.6
)

plt.xlabel("Age (Years)")
plt.ylabel("Ask Price")
plt.title("Age vs Ask Price")

plt.show()
#%% md
# Interpreting Scatter Plots

Scatter plots can reveal different types of relationships.

### Positive Relationship

As one variable increases, the other also tends to increase.

Example:

```
Price
 ^
 |          •
 |        •
 |      •
 |    •
 |  •
 +-------------------->
      Engine Size
```

Larger engines often correspond to higher prices.

---

### Negative Relationship

As one variable increases, the other decreases.

Example:

```
Price
 ^
 |  •
 |    •
 |      •
 |         •
 |            •
 +-------------------->
       Age
```

Higher mileage often corresponds to lower prices.

---

### No Relationship

Sometimes there is no clear pattern.

```
Price
 ^
 |  •     •
 |      •
 | •         •
 |      •
 |   •
 +-------------------->
      Mileage
```

In this case, one variable provides little information about the other.

---
#%% md
# Correlation

A **correlation** measures the **strength and direction** of a relationship between two numerical variables.

Correlation values range from:

```
-1  --------------------  +1
```

where:

| Value | Meaning |
|-------:|---------|
| +1 | Perfect positive correlation |
| 0 | No linear correlation |
| -1 | Perfect negative correlation |

---

# Examples

### Strong Positive Correlation

```
Correlation = +0.95
```

As one variable increases, the other almost always increases.

---

### Strong Negative Correlation

```
Correlation = -0.90
```

As one variable increases, the other usually decreases.

---

### Weak Correlation

```
Correlation = 0.10
```

There is almost no linear relationship.

---

# Pearson Correlation Coefficient

The most commonly used correlation measure is the **Pearson Correlation Coefficient**.

It measures the strength of a **linear relationship** between two numerical variables.

Its value always lies between:

```
-1 and +1
```

Interpretation:

| Pearson r | Interpretation |
|-----------|----------------|
| 0.90 to 1.00 | Very Strong Positive |
| 0.70 to 0.90 | Strong Positive |
| 0.40 to 0.70 | Moderate Positive |
| 0.00 to 0.40 | Weak Positive |
| 0 | No Linear Relationship |
| Negative Values | Same interpretation in the opposite direction |

---

# Calculating Pearson Correlation
#%%
df["Age"].corr(df["AskPrice"])

#%% md
Interpretation:

There is a **strong negative linear relationship** between mileage and price.

Cars with higher mileage generally have lower prices.

---
#%% md
# Correlation Matrix

When a dataset contains many numerical variables, calculating correlations one by one becomes inefficient.

A **Correlation Matrix** shows the correlation between every pair of numerical variables.

Example:

| | Price | Mileage | Engine |
|------|-------:|---------:|--------:|
| Price | 1.00 | -0.82 | 0.61 |
| Mileage | -0.82 | 1.00 | -0.25 |
| Engine | 0.61 | -0.25 | 1.00 |

The diagonal is always **1**, because every variable is perfectly correlated with itself.

---

# Creating a Correlation Matrix
#%%
print(df.info())
#%%
numeric_df = df.select_dtypes(include="number")

numeric_df.corr()
#%% md
---

# Visualizing the Correlation Matrix

A color map makes correlations easier to interpret.
#%%
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

corr = numeric_df.corr()

plt.figure(figsize=(8,6))

plt.imshow(corr, cmap="coolwarm")

plt.colorbar()

plt.xticks(range(len(corr.columns)), corr.columns, rotation=90)
plt.yticks(range(len(corr.columns)), corr.columns)

plt.title("Correlation Matrix")

plt.show()
#%% md
---

# Which Features Are Most Related to Price?

We can sort correlations with the target variable.
#%%
numeric_df.corr()["AskPrice"].sort_values(ascending=False)

#%% md


This tells us that:

- Engine Year has a positive relationship with price.
- Age has a negative relationship with price.

---

# Correlation Does NOT Mean Causation

This is one of the most important concepts in statistics and Machine Learning.

> **Just because two variables are correlated does NOT mean that one causes the other.**

Two variables may move together for many reasons.

---

## Example 1

Suppose we observe:

```
Ice Cream Sales ↑

and

Drowning Accidents ↑
```

Does eating ice cream cause drowning?

Of course not.

The hidden variable is:

```
Summer Temperature
```

During summer:

- More people buy ice cream.
- More people go swimming.

The two variables are correlated because they share a common cause.

---

## Example 2

Suppose expensive cars usually have larger engines.

Does engine size alone cause a higher price?

Not necessarily.

Other factors may also contribute:

- Brand
- Luxury features
- Technology
- Safety systems

Correlation alone cannot prove causation.

---

# Why Is Correlation Useful?

Although correlation cannot prove causation, it is still extremely useful.

Correlation helps us:

- Discover patterns
- Select useful features
- Remove redundant variables
- Understand datasets
- Build better Machine Learning models

It is often one of the first analyses performed during **Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)**.

---

# Classroom Exercise

Using the used car dataset:

1. Draw a scatter plot of **Mileage vs AskPrice**.
2. Compute the Pearson correlation coefficient.
3. Generate the correlation matrix.
4. Identify the variables that are most strongly correlated with **AskPrice**.

Discuss:

- Which variables appear to be useful predictors?
- Are there any surprising relationships?
- Can any of these correlations be interpreted as causal?

---

# Key Takeaways

- A **Scatter Plot** visualizes the relationship between two numerical variables.
- **Correlation** measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship.
- The **Pearson Correlation Coefficient** ranges from **-1 to +1**.
- A **Correlation Matrix** summarizes correlations among multiple numerical variables.
- **Correlation does not imply causation.**
- Correlation is a powerful tool for **Exploratory Data Analysis** and **Feature Selection**, but it should never be used alone to infer cause-and-effect relationships.

In the next section, we will explore **Statistical Inference**, where we learn how conclusions about an entire population can be drawn from a sample of data.
#%% md
# 6. Statistical Thinking

---

## Learning Objectives

By the end of this section, students will be able to:

- Understand the difference between a **population** and a **sample**.
- Explain the meaning of **statistical inference**.
- Understand why collecting data from an entire population is often impossible.
- Recognize how statistical thinking forms the foundation of Machine Learning.

---

# Why Do We Need Statistical Thinking?

Imagine you want to answer the following question:

> **"What is the average price of used cars in India?"**

Can you collect information about **every used car** in the country?

Probably not.

There are millions of cars, and new ones are bought and sold every day.

Instead, we collect information from a **small subset** of cars and use it to estimate the characteristics of all used cars.

This is the core idea behind **statistical thinking**.

---

# Population

A **population** is the **entire group** that we want to study.

Examples:

- Every used car in India
- Every customer of a bank
- Every student at a university
- Every house in a city

A population may contain thousands, millions, or even billions of observations.

---

# Sample

A **sample** is a **subset of the population**.

Instead of collecting data from everyone, we collect data from a smaller group.

Example:

Population

```
All Used Cars in India
≈ Millions of Cars
```

Sample

```
9,582 Cars
```

Our dataset contains information about **9,582 used cars**.

This dataset is a **sample**, not the entire population.

---

# Why Don't We Use the Entire Population?

In an ideal world, we would collect every observation.

In practice, this is rarely possible.

Some common reasons include:

- Too expensive
- Too time-consuming
- Data changes continuously
- Some observations are unavailable
- Privacy or legal restrictions

For example:

Suppose a bank has **20 million customers**.

Analyzing every customer's behavior every minute would require enormous computational resources.

Instead, analysts often work with representative samples.

---

# From Sample to Population

The goal of statistics is not simply to describe the sample.

The real goal is to **learn something about the population**.

For example, suppose our sample tells us:

- Average price = ₹750,000
- Median price = ₹680,000

We hope these values are also close to the true average and median of **all used cars** in the market.

---

# Statistical Inference

**Statistical inference** is the process of using information from a **sample** to make conclusions about the **population**.

In simple words:

> **We observe a small amount of data and make educated conclusions about a much larger group.**

This is one of the most important ideas in statistics.

---

# Example

Suppose we randomly select:

```
9,582 Used Cars
```

From this sample we calculate:

- Mean price
- Standard deviation
- Distribution
- Correlations

We then use these results to estimate what is likely true for **all used cars**.

We never claim absolute certainty.

Instead, we make conclusions based on evidence collected from the sample.

---

# Why Sampling Matters

Imagine selecting only luxury cars.

Would that represent the entire market?

No.

The average price would be much higher than reality.

Similarly, if we only sampled very old cars, the average price would be much lower.

A **good sample** should represent the population as accurately as possible.

This is why **random sampling** is important.

---
#%% md
# Statistical Thinking in Machine Learning

Machine Learning follows exactly the same philosophy.

When we train a model, we do **not** expect it to memorize the training data.

Instead, we want it to learn patterns that also work for **new, unseen data**.

Think about our dataset.

```
Population

All Used Cars
```

↓

```
Sample

9,582 Cars
```

↓

```
Machine Learning Model
```

↓

```
Predict Prices of Future Cars
```

The model learns from a sample but is expected to perform well on the entire population.

---

# Why Train/Test Split Makes Sense

This idea also explains why we split our dataset into:

- Training Set
- Test Set

The training set represents the data the model has already seen.

The test set simulates **new observations from the population**.

If the model performs well on unseen data, we gain confidence that it has learned general patterns rather than simply memorizing the training examples.

---
#%% md
# Statistics and Machine Learning

Statistics asks:

> **"What can we learn about a population from a sample?"**

Machine Learning asks:

> **"Can we learn patterns from a sample that generalize to unseen data?"**

Although the goals are different, both rely on the same fundamental principle:

> **Learning from limited data to make reliable predictions or conclusions about unseen observations.**

---

# Classroom Discussion

Our dataset contains **9,582 used cars**.

Discuss the following questions:

1. Is this dataset a **population** or a **sample**?
2. Why can't we collect information about every used car in India?
3. If the dataset contained only luxury cars, would our conclusions be reliable?
4. Why is it important for a sample to be representative of the population?
5. How is the Train/Test Split related to statistical inference?

---
#%% md
# Key Takeaways

- A **population** is the complete group we want to study.
- A **sample** is a subset of the population.
- In practice, we usually analyze samples because collecting the entire population is impractical.
- **Statistical inference** uses sample data to make conclusions about the population.
- Machine Learning is built on the same statistical foundation: learning patterns from a sample that generalize to unseen data.
- This is why evaluating models on unseen test data is essential—it measures how well our learned patterns extend beyond the training sample.

In the next section, we will use these statistical ideas to understand **why we split data into training and testing sets before building Machine Learning models**.
