# 7. Predictive Modeling Pipeline

---

## Learning Objectives

By the end of this section, students will be able to:

- Understand the overall workflow of a Machine Learning project.
- Identify the key components of a predictive modeling pipeline.
- Distinguish between **data**, **features**, **model**, **predictions**, and **evaluation**.
- See how all the concepts from today's session fit together.

---

# The Big Picture

So far, we have discussed:

- Loading data with **Pandas**
- Understanding variables
- Visualizing distributions
- Computing descriptive statistics
- Exploring relationships between variables
- Statistical thinking

But an important question remains:

> **How do these pieces come together to build a Machine Learning system?**

The answer is through the **Predictive Modeling Pipeline**.

---

# The Machine Learning Pipeline

Almost every supervised Machine Learning project follows the same high-level workflow.

```text
Raw Data
     │
     ▼
Feature Selection
     │
     ▼
Machine Learning Model
     │
     ▼
Predictions
     │
     ▼
Evaluation
```

Each step has a specific purpose.

---

# Step 1 — Data

Every Machine Learning project starts with **data**.

Data may come from:

- CSV files
- Databases
- APIs
- Sensors
- Images
- Text
- User activity logs

For this course, our data comes from a CSV file containing information about **9,582 used cars**.

Example:

| Brand | Year | Age | FuelType | AskPrice |
|--------|-----:|----:|----------|---------:|
| Honda | 2022 | 3 | Petrol | 850000 |
| Hyundai | 2020 | 5 | Diesel | 620000 |
| Tata | 2023 | 2 | CNG | 780000 |

Without data, Machine Learning is impossible.

---

# Step 2 — Features

A **feature** is an input variable used by the model to make predictions.

Examples from our dataset:

- Year
- Age
- FuelType
- Transmission
- kmDriven
- Owner

Suppose our goal is to predict the selling price of a car.

Then:

**Features (Inputs):**

```text
Age
kmDriven
FuelType
Transmission
```

**Target (Output):**

```text
AskPrice
```

In Machine Learning notation:

```text
Features  →  X

Target    →  y
```

---

# Step 3 — Machine Learning Model

A **model** is an algorithm that learns patterns from the training data.

The model receives:

- Features (`X`)
- Target (`y`)

During training, it tries to discover the relationship between them.

For example:

```
Age
Mileage
Fuel Type
Transmission
        │
        ▼
   Machine Learning Model
        │
        ▼
Predicted Price
```

In the next session, we will build our first model using **Linear Regression**.

---

# Step 4 — Prediction

Once the model has learned from the training data, it can make predictions for new observations.

For example, suppose we have a new car:

| Age | kmDriven | FuelType | Transmission |
|----:|----------:|----------|--------------|
| 3 | 45,000 | Petrol | Automatic |

The model predicts:

```text
Predicted AskPrice = ₹780,000
```

This prediction is based on the patterns learned during training.

---
#%% md
# Step 5 — Evaluation

A prediction is useful only if it is accurate.

Therefore, we compare the model's predictions with the actual values.

Example:

| Actual Price | Predicted Price |
|-------------:|----------------:|
| ₹800,000 | ₹780,000 |
| ₹650,000 | ₹670,000 |
| ₹520,000 | ₹510,000 |

The differences between actual and predicted values are called **errors**.

Evaluation tells us how well the model performs.

In the next session, we will introduce evaluation metrics such as:

- Mean Absolute Error (MAE)
- Mean Squared Error (MSE)

---

# Putting Everything Together

The complete workflow looks like this:

```text
Load Data
     │
     ▼
Explore and Understand Data
     │
     ▼
Select Features (X)
Define Target (y)
     │
     ▼
Train a Machine Learning Model
     │
     ▼
Make Predictions
     │
     ▼
Evaluate Performance
```

Notice that everything we learned in today's class belongs to the **data understanding** stage.

A good understanding of the data is the foundation of a successful Machine Learning model.

