#%% md
# 8. Train/Test Split

---

## Learning Objectives

By the end of this section, students will be able to:

- Understand why datasets are divided into training and testing sets.
- Explain the concept of **Data Leakage**.
- Understand the intuition behind **Overfitting**.
- Define the **Feature Matrix (X)** and **Target Vector (y)**.
- Split a dataset using `train_test_split()` from Scikit-Learn.

---

# Why Can't We Evaluate a Model on the Same Data?

Imagine a teacher gives students the exact questions that will appear on the final exam.

The students memorize every answer.

On exam day, everyone scores **100%**.

Does this mean they truly understand the subject?

**Probably not.**

They simply memorized the answers.

Machine Learning models can make exactly the same mistake.

---

# Memorization vs Learning

There is an important difference between:

**Memorizing**

```
"I have seen this exact example before."
```

and

**Learning**

```
"I understand the pattern and can solve new examples."
```

Our goal is **not** to build a model that memorizes the training data.

Our goal is to build a model that performs well on **new, unseen data**.

---

# The Problem

Suppose we train a model using **all 9,582 cars** in our dataset.

After training, we evaluate the model using those **same 9,582 cars**.

The model may report excellent performance.

But this tells us very little.

The model is being tested on data it has already seen.

---

# The Solution: Train/Test Split

To fairly evaluate a Machine Learning model, we divide the dataset into two parts.

```text
Entire Dataset
      │
      ▼
+--------------------+------------------+
|   Training Set     |    Testing Set   |
|       80%          |       20%        |
+--------------------+------------------+
```

The exact ratio may vary (e.g., 70/30, 80/20, or 90/10), but the idea is always the same.

---

# The Training Set

The **training set** is used to teach the model.

The model learns relationships such as:

```
Age
Year
Mileage
Fuel Type
Transmission
        │
        ▼
      AskPrice
```

The model is allowed to "see" only the training data.

---

# The Test Set

The **test set** is never shown during training.

It acts like a **final exam**.

After training is complete, we ask the model to make predictions for the test set.

Only then do we compare its predictions with the true values.

This gives us an unbiased estimate of how well the model performs on unseen data.

---

# Data Leakage

One of the biggest mistakes in Machine Learning is **Data Leakage**.

> **Data Leakage occurs when information from the test set is accidentally used during training.**

When this happens, the evaluation becomes overly optimistic.

The model appears to perform better than it actually will in real-world situations.

---

# Examples of Data Leakage

Some common examples include:

- Training on the entire dataset before creating the train/test split.
- Computing statistics (such as the mean or standard deviation) using the entire dataset before splitting.
- Accidentally including the target variable as one of the input features.
- Using information that would not be available at prediction time.

A good rule is:

> **The test set must remain completely untouched until the final evaluation.**

---

# Overfitting (First Introduction)

Another common problem is **Overfitting**.

An overfitted model learns the training data **too well**.

It memorizes details and even noise instead of learning general patterns.

As a result:

- Training performance is excellent.
- Test performance is poor.

---

# Example

Imagine a student who memorizes every practice question without understanding the concepts.

Training score:

```
100%
```

Final exam score:

```
62%
```

The student memorized instead of learning.

Machine Learning models can behave in exactly the same way.

---

# Why the Test Set Is Important

The test set helps us answer one important question:

> **Can the model generalize to new data?**

Generalization is one of the main goals of Machine Learning.

A model that performs well on unseen data is much more valuable than one that only memorizes the training examples.

---

# Defining Features (X) and Target (y)

Before splitting the data, we must separate:

- Input variables (**Features**)
- Output variable (**Target**)

For our used-car dataset:

**Features (X):**

- Year
- Age
- FuelType
- Transmission
- Owner

**Target (y):**

- AskPrice

In Python:

```python
X = df[[
    "Year",
    "Age"
]]

y = df["AskPrice"]
```

For simplicity, we will initially use only **Year** and **Age** as our features.

Later in the course, we will include additional variables and learn how to encode categorical features such as **FuelType** and **Transmission**.

---

# Splitting the Dataset

Scikit-Learn provides a convenient function for creating training and testing sets.
#%%
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split

X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(
    X,
    y,
    test_size=0.20,
    random_state=42
)
#%% md
---

# Understanding the Parameters

```python
test_size=0.20
```

Means:

```
80% → Training Set

20% → Test Set
```

---

```python
random_state=42
```

The dataset is shuffled before splitting.

Setting `random_state` ensures that everyone obtains the **same split**, making experiments reproducible.

This is especially useful for education, debugging, and scientific research.

---

# Checking the Split

We can verify the sizes of the datasets.
#%%
print(X_train.shape)
print(X_test.shape)

print(y_train.shape)
print(y_test.shape)
#%% md


Notice that:

- 7,665 observations are used for training.
- 1,917 observations are reserved for testing.

---

# The Complete Workflow

```text
Load Dataset
      │
      ▼
Select Features (X)
Select Target (y)
      │
      ▼
Train/Test Split
      │
      ▼
Train Model
      │
      ▼
Predict on Test Set
      │
      ▼
Evaluate Performance
```

This workflow is the foundation of nearly every supervised Machine Learning project.

---
#%% md
