### B. The Three Core Pillars of Machine Learning
### Supervised Learning:
The dataset contains explicit "answers" or targets ($y$). The model learns by comparing its predictions against these true answers.
- *Regression*: Predicting a continuous, infinite numerical value (e.g., predicting a car's price: 15,250 USD, 22,400 USD).
- *Classification*: Predicting a distinct, discrete category label (e.g., predicting if a car is "Reliable" or "Unreliable").
### Unsupervised Learning:
The dataset has no target column or labels. The algorithm parses the data to uncover hidden structures or natural clusters independently (e.g., grouping customers into distinct purchasing personas).
### Reinforcement Learning:
An autonomous agent learns to make sequential decisions within an environment by maximizing a system of cumulative rewards and minimizing penalties (e.g., an AI learning to drive a self-driving vehicle).
