# 2. Machine Learning Workflow

---

## Learning Objectives

By the end of this section, students will be able to:

- Understand the end-to-end lifecycle of a Machine Learning project.
- Identify the major stages from data collection to deployment.
- Recognize that training a model is only one step in the overall workflow.
- Appreciate that most of the effort in real-world Machine Learning projects is spent before model training.

---

# The Big Picture

Many beginners believe that Machine Learning is simply:

```
Data
   ↓
Algorithm
   ↓
Prediction
```

In reality, building a Machine Learning system involves many stages.

Training the model is only **one step** in a much larger process.

---

# End-to-End Machine Learning Workflow

```text
Collect Data
      │
      ▼
Clean Data
      │
      ▼
Feature Engineering
      │
      ▼
Train/Test Split
      │
      ▼
Train Model
      │
      ▼
Evaluate Model
      │
      ▼
Deploy Model
```

Let's briefly examine each stage.

---

# Step 1 — Collect Data

Every Machine Learning project begins with collecting data.

Data may come from:

- CSV files
- Databases
- APIs
- Sensors
- Mobile applications
- Websites
- User activity logs

For this course, we use a public dataset containing **9,582 used cars**.

Without data, there is nothing to learn.

---

# Step 2 — Clean Data

Real-world data is rarely perfect.

Before training a model, we often need to clean the dataset.

Typical cleaning tasks include:

- Handling missing values
- Removing duplicate records
- Fixing incorrect data types
- Removing unnecessary characters
- Correcting inconsistent values

Example:

```text
Original Value

₹ 1,95,000
```

↓

```text
195000
```

Another example:

```text
kmDriven

"45,000 km"
```

↓

```text
45000
```

This process is called **Data Cleaning** or **Data Preprocessing**.

---

# Step 3 — Feature Engineering

After cleaning the data, we prepare the input variables for the model.

This process is called **Feature Engineering**.

It includes tasks such as:

- Selecting useful features
- Creating new features
- Encoding categorical variables
- Scaling numerical variables

Example:

Instead of using only:

```
Year
```

we create:

```
Age = Current Year − Year
```

Age is often more informative than the manufacturing year itself.

Another example:

```
FuelType

Petrol
Diesel
CNG
```

must eventually be converted into numerical values before many Machine Learning algorithms can use it.

Good features often lead to better models.

---

# Step 4 — Train/Test Split

Before training the model, we divide the dataset into:

- Training Set
- Test Set

```text
Dataset

↓

80% Training

20% Testing
```

The model learns only from the training data.

The test data remains unseen until the final evaluation.

This allows us to measure how well the model generalizes to new data.

---

# Step 5 — Train the Model

Now we choose a Machine Learning algorithm.

Examples include:

- Linear Regression
- K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)
- Logistic Regression
- Naive Bayes

The algorithm analyzes the training data and learns patterns that relate the input features to the target variable.

This process is called **training**.

---

# Step 6 — Evaluate the Model

After training, we evaluate the model using the test set.

The model makes predictions for data it has never seen before.

We then compare:

```
Actual Values

↓

Predicted Values
```

The closer the predictions are to the true values, the better the model.

In the next chapters, we will learn evaluation metrics such as:

- Mean Absolute Error (MAE)
- Mean Squared Error (MSE)
- Accuracy

---

# Step 7 — Deploy the Model

A successful model should eventually be used in a real application.

This step is called **deployment**.

Examples include:

- Predicting used-car prices on an online marketplace
- Detecting fraudulent bank transactions
- Recommending movies on a streaming platform
- Filtering spam emails

After deployment, the model continues making predictions for new users.

---

# The Machine Learning Lifecycle

The workflow is not a straight line.

Machine Learning projects are iterative.

```text
Collect Data
      │
      ▼
Clean Data
      │
      ▼
Feature Engineering
      │
      ▼
Train
      │
      ▼
Evaluate
      │
      ▼
Good Performance?
      │
   Yes │ No
      │
      ▼
 Deploy
      ▲
      │
Improve Features
Collect More Data
Choose Another Model
```

If the model performs poorly, we improve the data, features, or algorithm and repeat the process.

---

# Where Do Data Scientists Spend Their Time?

Many people assume that most of the work is training models.

In practice, a large portion of a data scientist's time is spent on data preparation.

A common estimate is:

| Stage | Approximate Time |
|--------|-----------------:|
| Data Collection & Cleaning | 40–50% |
| Feature Engineering | 10–30% |
| Model Training | 10–40% |
| Evaluation & Deployment | Remaining Time |

This is why understanding data is so important.

---

# Classroom Discussion

Imagine you are asked to build a system that predicts apartment prices.

Discuss the following questions:

1. Where would you collect the data?
2. What cleaning steps might be necessary?
3. Which features would be useful?
4. Why should the data be split before training?
5. How would you know whether the model is good enough to deploy?

---

# Key Takeaways

- Machine Learning is an **end-to-end process**, not just model training.
- The typical workflow consists of:
  1. Collect Data
  2. Clean Data
  3. Feature Engineering
  4. Train/Test Split
  5. Train the Model
  6. Evaluate the Model
  7. Deploy the Model
- Data preparation is often the most time-consuming stage of a Machine Learning project.
- Machine Learning development is **iterative**: when performance is unsatisfactory, we improve the data, features, or model and repeat the process.
- Understanding this workflow provides the roadmap for everything students will learn throughout the remainder of the course.
